From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:27:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: Select the PCIe core before testing whether
 the chip kept its state

On resume the driver reads the PCIe core's interrupt mask to decide
whether the firmware survived the sleep: non-zero means a hot resume, zero
a chip that was reset and has to be probed again. The register is read
through the BAR0 window, and which core that window shows is set by a
vendor register in configuration space that the PCI core does not save.
After a sleep in which the chip was reset, for instance because its
PCIe port was powered off, the window is back at its default, and the
test reads a register of another core. If that is non-zero the driver
takes the hot path against a firmware that is gone.

Select the PCIe core first.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
index c103f618e..9c52f50d8 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -2710,6 +2710,13 @@ static int brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3(struct device *dev)
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The BAR0 window register is not part of the configuration space the
+	 * PCI core restores. After a sleep that reset the chip it selects
+	 * another core, and the test below would read that core's register.
+	 */
+	brcmf_pcie_select_core(devinfo, BCMA_CORE_PCIE2);
+
 	/* Check if device is still up and running, if so we are ready */
 	if (brcmf_pcie_read_reg32(devinfo, devinfo->reginfo->intmask) != 0) {
 		brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Try to wakeup device....\n");
